@startuml

title Approval request expiry: the sweep

participant "Member" as member
participant "IAM" as iam
participant "Scheduler" as sched
participant "Inbox" as inbox
participant "Variability" as var
participant "Administrator" as admin
database "Postgres" as db

== The ask carries its deadline ==

member -> iam : iam.v1.ops.ask_for_roles
iam -> inbox : inbox.v1.ops.raise_approval\n(kind iam.role_grant)
inbox -> db : read the kind
db --> inbox : expires_after_days = 14
note right of inbox
  The kind holds the policy, so the
  request states when it stops waiting:
  expires_at = now + the kind's days.
end note
inbox -> db : write the request (waiting, expires_at)
inbox -> admin : inbox.approval_waiting
note over member, admin
  Nobody answers. The request stays open
  and the queue keeps offering it.
end note

== The inbox declares the job and reads when to fire it ==

inbox -> var : variability.v1.ops.get_setting\n(inbox.approval_expiry.schedule)
var -> db : read the setting
db --> var : the cron expression
var --> inbox : the value and its declared type
note right of inbox
  That the sweep recurs is the inbox's
  own behaviour, so the inbox registers
  the job. How often is the installation's
  answer, so the schedule is a system
  setting. The inbox asks variability for
  it by name, over the wire and with the
  permission to read it, rather than
  reaching into variability's tables.
end note
inbox -> sched : scheduler.v1.job_definitions.list
sched --> inbox : the jobs, so the inbox\nfinds the one it registered last time
inbox -> sched : scheduler.v1.job_definitions.put\n(name ores.inbox.approval_expiry,\naction_type nats_publish, this subject)
note right of inbox
  The job is recognised by name and the
  registration reuses the identifier of
  the job already carrying it, so a
  restart replaces the row instead of
  colliding with it. A registration that
  cannot be made stops the service: an
  inbox that is up with no expiry job is
  worse than one that is down and says so.
end note

== The scheduled sweep ==

sched -> inbox : inbox.v1.ops.expire_overdue_approvals
note right of sched
  A job in the catalogue belongs to no
  tenant and reaches the service, not
  the store: telling the asker is part
  of the act, and the function alone
  tells nobody.
end note
inbox -> db : ores_inbox_expire_approval_requests_fn(actor)
note right of db
  Security definer, so it reads every
  tenant. An open request whose
  expires_at has passed becomes
  expired, and each closed request is
  answered as one row.
end note
db --> inbox : one row per request closed
inbox -> member : inbox.approval_expired

== The decider who arrives too late ==

admin -> inbox : inbox.v1.ops.decide_approval
inbox -> db : ores_inbox_decide_approval_request_fn(...)
db --> inbox : conflict: the request expired
note right of db
  The same rule, applied when a decider
  reaches for the request rather than on
  the sweep. Both paths leave it expired
  and neither decides it.
end note

@enduml
